            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable news on NASA’s Mars Perseverance rover is:\n\n- **Perseverance has reached a “marathon” milestone on Mars**: it has now driven **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** across the Martian surface. NASA says it hit that distance on **sol 1,890** of the mission, after about **five years and four months** of driving—less than half the time Opportunity took to reach the same distance.【6:0†source】\n\n- **NASA released orbital imagery of the milestone**: the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter’s HiRISE camera captured Perseverance as a tiny speck on the surface, along with its tracks, shortly before it reached the marathon distance. At the time, the rover was operating west of Jezero Crater in an area nicknamed **“Arbot.”**【6:2†source】\n\n- **New science from Jezero’s rim**: NASA reports that Perseverance found evidence that a **245-foot / 75-meter-thick** stack of ancient rock on Jezero Crater’s rim—called the **“Broom Point member”**—was built by repeated asteroid impacts. The rocks are thought to be **more than 3.9 billion years old**, offering a record of early Martian impact history.【6:1†source】\n\nIn short: Perseverance is still active, has just logged a major driving milestone, and is producing new geological insights from ancient terrain around Jezero Crater.",
                "annotations": [
